    "q-126899": {
      "dom_id": "step-q126899",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨-\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3},2t-1⟩,$ find all of the points at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $⟨-1,-1⟩.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2{t}^{2},2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $⟨-1,-1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $⟨-1,-1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨-1,-1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨-1,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨-2{t}^{2},2⟩ & =0 \\\\ (-1)(-2{t}^{2})+(-1)(2) & =0 \\\\ 2{t}^{2}-2 & =0 \\\\ 2(t+1)(t-1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $t=\\pm 1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨-\\frac{2(-1)^{3}}{3},2(-1)-1⟩=⟨\\frac{2}{3},-3⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨-\\frac{2(1)^{3}}{3},2(1)-1⟩=⟨-\\frac{2}{3},1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨-1,-1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the points $(-\\frac{2}{3},1)$ and $(\\frac{2}{3},-3).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
