    "q-164834": {
      "dom_id": "step-q164834",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}+1\\,,\\,2t-1⟩,$ find all of the points at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $⟨1\\,,\\,-2⟩.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1)\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t\\,,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors  $⟨1\\,,\\,-2⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $⟨1\\,,\\,-2⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨1\\,,\\,-2⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨1\\,,\\,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨2t\\,,\\,2⟩ & =0 \\\\ 1\\cdot 2t+(-2)\\cdot 2 & =0 \\\\ 2t-4 & =0 \\\\ 2(t-2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=2.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =⟨{(2)}^{2}+1\\,,\\,2(2)-1⟩=⟨5\\,,\\,3⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨1\\,,\\,-2⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(5,3).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
