    "q-165041": {
      "dom_id": "step-q165041",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{4}+4t\\,,\\,{t}^{3}-2t\\,,\\,2{t}^{3}-3t⟩,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}+4t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-3t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4{t}^{3}+4,\\,3{t}^{2}-2,\\,6{t}^{2}-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c\\,⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c\\,⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩ \\\\ ⟨4{t}^{3}+4,\\,3{t}^{2}-2,\\,6{t}^{2}-3⟩ & =c\\,⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩ \\\\ ⟨4{t}^{3}+4,\\,3{t}^{2}-2,\\,6{t}^{2}-3⟩ & =⟨0\\,,\\,c\\,,\\,3c⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4{t}^{3}+4=0 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-2=c \\\\ 6{t}^{2}-3=3c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain that $t=-1.$ Substituting to the second equation, we get $c=1.$\nThese values also satisfy the third equation. \n\nTherefore, $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=-1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨(-1)^{4}+4(-1)\\,,\\,(-1)^{3}-2(-1)\\,,\\,2(-1)^{3}-3(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,\\,1,\\,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(-3,1,1).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
